LEAD = {
    "headline": "YOUTUBE NOW SAYS BOOSTING A CREATOR'S VIDEO LIFTS CONVERSIONS 30 PERCENT.  IN MAY IT SAID 20, FOOTNOTED TO THE IDENTICAL TWELVE MONTHS OF DATA, AND NEITHER NUMBER HAS EVER CARRIED A SAMPLE SIZE",
    "deck": "The claimed effect went up by half while the data behind it stayed exactly where it was.  The new figure does not appear in the 25-page pack it promotes, only in the post promoting it.  And the one study in the whole document with a published sample measures what people remembered and said they intended, not what anybody bought.",
    "stamps": [
        ("PPC LAND · 15 OCT", "https://ppc.land/youtubes-creator-boost-lift-claim-rises-to-30-from-20-on-the-same-data/"),
        ("MARKETING DIVE · 15 OCT", "https://www.marketingdive.com/news/why-brands-struggle-with-influencer-measurement-and-what-to-do-about-it/832380/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Two bits of vocabulary first, because the whole item turns on them.  Creator Partnerships Boost is the product where you put advertising money behind a creator's existing video, so it reaches people beyond the creator's own audience.  You are not making a new ad.  You are buying distribution for a video that already exists.  Conversion lift is meant to be the extra conversions the advertising caused, measured against a group that did not see it — the word lift is doing a lot of work, because it implies somebody ran a holdout.",
        "Here is what changed on Wednesday.  PPC Land's Luis Rijo noticed that a post from the YouTube for Business page now states that <mark>Creator Partnerships Boost delivered an average 30 percent increase in conversion lift</mark> for Demand Gen campaigns, while holding steady what it costs to acquire one customer.  The footnote under it reads \"Google Internal Data, Global, Jan 2025 - Jan 2026\".  In May, Google's own playbook put the same product at <mark>an average 20 percent increase in conversion lift</mark>, footnoted \"Google Data, Global, Jan 2025 - Jan 2026\".  Same twelve months.  Fifty percent more effect.  As recently as 23 September, Google was still citing 20.",
        "Then it gets worse rather than better.  The 30 percent figure <mark>does not appear anywhere in the body of the 25-page PDF</mark> it is being used to promote — it exists only in the social post.  Neither the 20 nor the 30 carries a sample size, a market list or a methodology note.  Nothing in the pack defines what a conversion is or what the lift is measured against.  The PDF itself carries no publication date, and its most recent footnoted data runs to 1 September 2026.",
        "There are perfectly legitimate reasons a number moves.  The set of campaigns in the average could have changed.  The definition of a conversion could have changed.  The weighting could have changed, or the treatment of outliers.  Any one of those is an ordinary methodological revision and nobody would blink at it.  None of them is disclosed, and neither document acknowledges that the earlier figure existed.",
        "There is also a reason nobody pushes back hard on figures like this one.  The Association of National Advertisers reported yesterday that <mark>67 percent of marketers call measurement the hardest part of creator work</mark>, and the cause it gives is that platforms define views, reach, engagement and video completion differently, with nothing in the market accounting for the same person being counted on three channels at once.  When there is no agreed definition of anything, an undefined number is not wrong.  It is unfalsifiable, which is a much more comfortable place for it to sit.",
        "The one measurement in the pack you can actually grade is Kantar's, and it is worth reading carefully because it is the honest one.  Commissioned by Google: <mark>20 brand campaigns across 10 verticals, United States, 2026, mobile, adults 25 to 54, 4,342 respondents split 2,259 control and 2,083 exposed, 95 percent confidence</mark>.  It reports brand plus creator content against brand content alone at 1.6 times aided brand awareness, 1.5 times consideration and 1.6 times purchase intent.  Those are brand metrics.  They are not conversions, and they are not the 30 percent.  The chart's own footnote still contains the unfilled drafting placeholders X and Y where two comparison figures are supposed to go.",
        "Two claims quietly left the building between May and October.  The May edition led on <mark>an 86 percent higher incremental long-term return than paid social</mark>, from a Circana study Google commissioned — gone from the October version, with no explanation.  So has a Kantar figure of 5 percent higher long-term brand equity, which came from the identical 4,342-person dataset now being used to support different numbers.  One commissioned study, two headline findings, two editions, never published as a standalone paper.",
        "And the figure on the pack's opening page, the one that travels furthest, is the softest of the lot.  Viewers are \"13x more likely to search and 5x more likely to buy\" when a YouTube creator talks about your product.  The footnote narrows it to traffic observational data across about 60 brands, globally, December 2025 — and to organic videos about a brand, not sponsored creator content at all.  Observational means it compares people who watched with people who did not, which cannot separate the effect of the video from the fact that people already interested in a brand are the people most likely to watch a video about it.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("30%", "the October claim for conversion lift from boosting a creator's video"),
        ("20%", "the May claim, footnoted to the identical twelve months of data"),
        ("4,342", "people in the only study in the pack with a published sample size"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "One outlet.  PPC Land is the only publication to have reported this, and the social post and the PDF are described in its piece rather than linked, so every quoted string here is Rijo's reading of two documents we have not seen ourselves.  Google has not responded publicly and has not been asked for comment on the record in any coverage we can find.  Kantar's study was paid for by Google and has never been published as a standalone paper, so the 4,342-person sample and the control split are disclosures inside a marketing document rather than a method anyone can audit.  The ANA finding used below for context rests on 78 self-selected respondents.",
    "so_what": "A lift figure with no sample size is not a measurement, it is a marketing asset, and this one has now behaved like one in public — it went up by half while the data under it did not move.  The product being sold is genuinely interesting, because putting money behind a creator's own video is a different buy from recutting the footage into a brand ad, and that distinction has held up in independent work.  But the evidence you are handed to justify it cannot be checked, graded or compared with anything, and the single number in the pack that does come with a control group measures intent rather than purchases.",
    "do_this": "Email your Google representative this week and ask for three things in writing: the sample size behind the 30 percent, the definition of conversion lift it is measured against, and the reason the same January 2025 to January 2026 dataset read 20 percent in May.  If you put the 20 percent figure into a business case this year, put the new one beside it in your next finance conversation and show both.  Build your actual case for creator boost on the Kantar numbers instead, and say out loud that they measure brand awareness and purchase intent rather than purchases.",
}

